  "nl_text": "This schema, named schemabench_0148, is a database for an e-commerce website. It contains eight tables, each representing a different aspect of the website's functionality.\nThe first table, orders, stores information about each order placed by a customer. It has columns for the order's unique ID, the customer's ID, the total amount of the order, the order's status, the date and time the order was created, and the date and time the order was last updated. The table also has a foreign key that references the customers table, allowing for the association of an order with a specific customer.\nThe second table, payments, stores information about each payment made for an order. It has columns for the payment's unique ID, the invoice ID, the amount paid, the date and time the payment was made, the payment method, and the date and time the payment was last updated. The table also has a foreign key that references the invoices table, allowing for the association of a payment with a specific invoice.\nThe third table, refunds, stores information about each refund made for an order. It has columns for the refund's unique ID, the order ID, the amount refunded, the reason for the refund, the date and time the refund was made, and the date and time the refund was last updated. The table also has a foreign key that references the orders table, allowing for the association of a refund with a specific order.\nThe fourth table, order_items, stores information about each item in an order. It has columns for the item's unique ID, the order ID, the product ID, the quantity of the item, the price of the item, and the date and time the item was last updated. The table also has foreign keys that reference the products and orders tables, allowing for the association of an order item with a specific product and order.\nThe fifth table, products, stores information about each product offered on the website. It has columns for the product's unique ID, the product's name, the category ID, the price of the product, the stock of the product, a description of the product, and the date and time the product was last updated. The table also has a foreign key that references the categories table, allowing for the association of a product with a specific category.\nThe sixth table, categories, stores information about each category of product offered on the website. It has columns for the category's unique ID, the category's name, a description of the category, and the date and time the category was last updated.\nThe seventh table, users, stores information about each user of the website. It has columns for the user's unique ID, the user's name, the user's email address, the date and time the user's email was verified, the user's password, a remember token, and the date and time the user was last updated.\nThe eighth table, invoices, stores information about each invoice issued for an order. It has columns for the invoice's unique ID, the order ID, the amount of the invoice, the date and time the invoice was issued, the date and time the invoice is due, the status of the invoice, and the date and time the invoice was last updated. The table also has a foreign key that references the orders table, allowing for the association of an invoice with a specific order.\nIn summary, this schema is a comprehensive database for an e-commerce website, containing tables for orders, payments, refunds, order items, products, categories, users, and invoices. Each table has its own unique columns and foreign keys, allowing for the association of data across different aspects of the website's functionality.",
  "gold_question": "Online Accounting Software for Small Businesses and Freelancers\n\n1) Background / Context\n\n   Akaunting is an online accounting software solution tailored for small businesses and freelancers. It leverages modern technologies including Laravel, VueJS, Tailwind, and RESTful APIs. The software features a modular structure that supports an App Store, catering to both users and developers for expanded functionality.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n   - To provide a comprehensive accounting platform that enables small businesses and freelancers to manage financial operations efficiently.\n   - To offer a scalable and customizable solution through an integrated App Store.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n   - Stakeholders: Small business owners, freelancers, developers, and the Akaunting management team.\n   - Users: End-users including small business owners and freelancers who require accounting services, and developers looking to enhance the platform’s functionalities.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n   - User Management: Allow users to register, manage their profile details, and authenticate securely.\n   - Order Management: Users can create and manage orders with associated items, track order statuses, and compute total amounts.\n   - Product Management: Facilitate the management of products, including adding, updating product details, monitoring stock levels, and categorizing products.\n   - Invoice Processing: Generate and manage invoices linked to orders, with capabilities to track due dates and payment statuses.\n   - Payment and Refund Handling: Process payments and log refunds associated with invoices and orders.\n   - Financial Reporting: Generate reports and summaries of sales, payments, and overall financial performance.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n   - Performance: The system must handle multiple concurrent users with efficient response times.\n   - Security: Ensure data privacy and secure handling of sensitive financial information and user credentials.\n   - Scalability: Provide a modular architecture to facilitate easy integration of new features via the App Store.\n   - Usability: Design a user-friendly interface with intuitive navigation and clear access to all major functionalities.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0148.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0148\nCREATE TABLE `orders` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `total_amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `orders_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payments` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `invoice_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount_paid` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `payment_method` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `payments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`invoice_id`) REFERENCES `invoices` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `refunds` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `order_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount_refunded` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `reason` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `refund_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `refunds_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`order_id`) REFERENCES `orders` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `order_items` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `order_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `product_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `order_items_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`order_id`) REFERENCES `orders` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `order_items_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`product_id`) REFERENCES `products` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `products` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `stock` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `products_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `categories` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email_verified_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `remember_token` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invoices` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `order_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `issued_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `due_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invoices_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`order_id`) REFERENCES `orders` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
